# ─── APEX Production Intelligence Stack ─────────────────────────────────────
# packages/core/src/llm-client.ts is the source of truth.
# All APEX units route through OpenRouter.
#
# ZERO-COST production LLM order (exhaustion pauses APEX; there is no paid fallback):
#   1. nex-agi/nex-n2.5-mini:free
#   2. nex-agi/nex-n2.5-pro:free
#   3. nvidia/nemotron-3-super-120b-a12b:free
#   4. nvidia/nemotron-3.5-lightning:free
#   5. openrouter/free
#   6. nvidia/nemotron-3-ultra-550b-a55b:free
#
# Three independent qualifying OpenRouter accounts (~1,000 :free requests/day each,
# ~3,000/day combined) are load-balanced by key fingerprint. Multiple env names
# for the same account still share one bucket.

OPENROUTER_FREE_API_KEY=
# Optional additional independent-account keys. Account-level balance/rate limits
# still apply when two names hold keys for the same OpenRouter account.
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
OPENROUTER_API_KEY_2=
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY_3 is burned (100% live failures) and is not a roster member.
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY_4=

# Optional OpenRouter MANAGEMENT keys — one per independent account. These
# cannot run inference. They list (and, by operator action only, create/rotate)
# inference keys so APEX can prove each live key belongs to a distinct account.
# Bind as GitHub / Cloud Run secrets. Never commit the values. Never paste them
# into chat logs.
# OPENROUTER_MGMT_KEY=
# OPENROUTER_MGMT_KEY_2=
# OPENROUTER_MGMT_KEY_3=
# OPENROUTER_MGMT_KEY_4=

# ─── Admin authentication ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required for dashboard/API login. Configure these as deployment secrets;
# there is deliberately no credential fallback in source.
APEX_ADMIN_PASSWORD=
APEX_ADMIN_TOKEN=

# ─── Agent Behavior ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# strict = gate every agent; normal = role/tool policy (recommended).
APEX_APPROVAL_MODE=normal

# ─── Production Resource Governor ────────────────────────────────────────────
# 0 = no APEX-wide daily token ceiling. Set a positive value when you want a
# deliberate workspace spend ceiling; OpenRouter account controls remain the
# authoritative billing limit.
APEX_TOKEN_CAP_TOTAL=0
# Optional comma-separated caps, e.g. provider:tokens. Leave unset unless you
# want a secondary token ceiling. Inference remains $0; exhaustion pauses APEX.
APEX_TOKEN_PACING_ENABLED=true
APEX_TOKEN_PACING_BURST_TOKENS=50000

# ─── Daily REQUEST budget ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Separate from the token caps above, and usually the one that actually binds.
# OpenRouter's free allowance is rationed per REQUEST — a fixed number of calls
# per account per UTC day whatever their size — so a workspace can sit far under
# every token cap it owns and still be refused.
#
# 2,900 sits under a three-account free ceiling of 3,000/day (1,000 per account
# once a $10 deposit lifts each off the ~200/day base tier) with headroom for
# requests this ledger cannot see, such as a second revision mid-rollout.
# Set 0 to disable the budget entirely. Only raise this after /health
# providerCredits.uniqueAccounts confirms that many independent OpenRouter users.
APEX_REQUEST_CAP_TOTAL=2000

# ─── Daily PAID spend budget ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Dollars, not requests: paid inference is rationed by money and consumes none
# of the free request allowance above, so the two budgets are independent.
# When this is spent the paid rung DROPS OUT of the routing order and APEX
# continues on free models alone — a graceful fallback, not an outage.
# 0 disables paid spend entirely (free-only).
# At DeepSeek V4 Flash list price ($0.06/M in, $0.12/M out) $2/day buys roughly
# 1,000-3,300 requests depending on prompt size; the ledger reports the real
# figure on /health rather than relying on this estimate.
APEX_DAILY_SPEND_USD=2.00
# Paid routing also requires the explicit operator opt-in below. Both are
# needed: the switch says "paid is allowed", the budget says "this much".
# APEX_PAID_FALLBACK=confirmed
APEX_SPEND_PACING_ENABLED=true
# Released at 00:00 UTC so a restart can do paid work at once without a busy
# hour taking the whole day's budget.
APEX_SPEND_PACING_BURST_USD=0.15
# Pacing spreads the cap across the UTC day instead of letting the workforce
# spend it all before noon. This is what makes agents wait between turns; it is
# also what makes an exhausted budget a brief pause rather than a dead day.
APEX_REQUEST_PACING_ENABLED=true
# Released immediately at 00:00 UTC so a restart can do real work at once.
APEX_REQUEST_PACING_BURST=150
# Short-window rate limit, and NOT redundant with the daily cap: the ramp above
# bounds the day's total but not its rate, so a deploy at 17:20 UTC starts with
# ~2,000 requests already accrued and unspent. On 2026-09-12 that let APEX issue
# 1,388 requests in 26 minutes, trip OpenRouter's per-minute limiter, and park
# every provider until the UTC reset — under budget for the day, total outage in
# practice. 15/min sits below the typical free-tier ceiling with headroom for
# the retry a failure triggers. 0 disables it.
APEX_REQUEST_RATE_PER_MIN=15
# MORE ACCOUNTS IS THE ONLY WAY TO BUY MORE FREE THROUGHPUT. OpenRouter's free
# allowance is a per-ACCOUNT daily request budget shared across every :free model
# at once, not a per-model limit — so adding more free MODELS buys nothing, while
# adding a key for another account buys a whole extra 1,000/day. Set this to a
# fourth account's key and load balancing picks it up with no other change.
# Deliberately NOT added to the paid credential list: the $10 deposit on an
# account exists to lift its free tier from ~200/day to 1,000/day, and spending
# that balance on tokens is how the paid chain hit HTTP 402 on 2026-09-12.
# OPENROUTER_API_KEY_4=

# Optional per-ACCOUNT caps. Written against env var names, but resolved to the
# account each key belongs to — APEX reads OpenRouter keys from five env names
# across three real accounts, so names holding the same key are ONE account and
# the strictest cap among them applies. Check the `accounts[]` labels on /health
# to see which names resolve together before setting these.
# An account that hits its cap steps aside while the others keep serving.
# APEX_REQUEST_CAPS=OPENROUTER_API_KEY:1000,OPENROUTER_API_KEY_2:1000

# Agents are instructed to batch independent tool calls into one reply, which
# only works if the request permits more than one call per turn. Sent ONLY to
# models whose OpenRouter catalog entry advertises parallel_tool_calls -- no
# free model currently does, so on the free-only chain this is inert and each
# tool round trip costs its own request. Set to `off` to disable it entirely.
APEX_PARALLEL_TOOL_CALLS=on

# Six process-wide inference slots across the 13-agent workforce. Lead research
# may use at most three by default; all requests still respect provider pacing,
# circuit breakers, retry-after, and token reservations.
APEX_MAX_CONCURRENT_LLM_CALLS=6
APEX_LEAD_RESEARCH_CONCURRENCY=3
APEX_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS=4096
# APEX_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS_CEO=8192
# APEX_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS_CTO=8192

APEX_SELF_REVIEW=adaptive

# Experimental only. Keep false while the primary production database/runtime
# remains outside the unfinished Convex migration.
APEX_CONVEX_AUTONOMY_ENABLED=false

# ─── Google Cloud Run deployment ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Fail-closed by default. Setting project/region/service does not itself permit
# a deploy; APEX_DEPLOY_ENABLED must also explicitly authorize the environment.
APEX_DEPLOY_ENABLED=off
APEX_GCP_PROJECT_ID=
APEX_CLOUD_RUN_REGION=
APEX_CLOUD_RUN_SERVICE=
# Optional if Cloud Build uses a regional endpoint.
# APEX_CLOUD_BUILD_REGION=
APEX_DEPLOY_HEALTH_URL=https://apex.donmatthews.live
# Optional clean checkout path used by the deployment CLI.
# APEX_DEPLOY_SOURCE_DIR=/path/to/Apex

# ─── Workspace / Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# WORKSPACE_ROOT=/path/to/workspace
DATABASE_URL=postgres://USER:PASSWORD@HOST:5432/apex
DB_LOGGING=false

# ─── Durable artifact store (autonomous-execution scheduler, Phase 1) ─────────
# GCS bucket in the existing GCP project/region (gcloud storage buckets create
# gs://<name> --location=<region>). Names only — real values are operator
# configuration, never guessed. When unset every artifact/workspace tool fails
# closed with an actionable error; the control plane keeps running.
APEX_ARTIFACT_BUCKET=
# Sandbox executor (Phase 4): Cloud Run Jobs resource name. Dispatch loop is a
# no-op until set.
APEX_EXECUTOR_JOB=
# Workstream repo org used by create_github_repo (default patriotnewsactivism).
# APEX_GITHUB_ORG=patriotnewsactivism
# Dynamic-job governance (Phase 5): ceiling for agent-created crons (min 5).
# APEX_MAX_DYNAMIC_JOBS=25
# Executor tuning.
# EXECUTOR_MAX_TURNS=40
# EXECUTOR_TASK_HARD_TIMEOUT_MS=3300000
# EXECUTOR_ALLOW_RUNSHELL=false

# ─── Checkpoint/resume and approval yield (ADR-014) ───────────────────────────
# Fraction of the hard task timeout at which a task must checkpoint and yield
# instead of continuing to burn its execution slot. Clamped to [0.3, 0.95].
# APEX_SOFT_TIMEOUT_RATIO=0.7
# Ordinary (non-executor-job) hard wall-clock timeout override, in ms. The
# executor-job ceiling is EXECUTOR_TASK_HARD_TIMEOUT_MS above.
# APEX_TASK_HARD_TIMEOUT_MS=600000
# How long a gated approval may sit unanswered before it is durably
# auto-rejected (never auto-approved). 0 disables the expiry entirely.
# APEX_APPROVAL_AUTO_REJECT_HOURS=24

# ─── Base44 Superagent Connector (optional) ──────────────────────────────────
# Server-side secret only. Never expose this with VITE_/NEXT_PUBLIC_ prefixes.
# Rotate any key that has been pasted into chat, logs, screenshots, or source.
BASE44_SUPERAGENT_API_KEY=
BASE44_SUPERAGENT_ID=6a515f4e071e32fc10378575
BASE44_SUPERAGENT_TIMEOUT_MS=90000

# ─── BuildMyBot Connector (optional) ─────────────────────────────────────────
# BUILDMYBOT_SUPABASE_URL=
# BUILDMYBOT_SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY=
# BUILDMYBOT_APP_URL=https://www.buildmybot.app
# BUILDMYBOT_CRON_SECRET=
# BUILDMYBOT_RAILWAY_TOKEN=
# BUILDMYBOT_RAILWAY_SERVICE_ID=60b6d260-f5d8-463d-87be-58339545eaaf
# BUILDMYBOT_RAILWAY_ENVIRONMENT_ID=6ce38db0-789b-4fe9-ad02-f068fe6866ae

# ─── CaseBuddy Connector (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# CASEBUDDY_SUPABASE_URL=
# CASEBUDDY_SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY=
# CASEBUDDY_APP_URL=https://casebuddy.live
# CASEBUDDY_SYSTEM_USER_ID=

# ─── TubeScribe Connector (optional, read-only monitoring) ───────────────────
# TUBESCRIBE_SUPABASE_URL=
# TUBESCRIBE_SUPABASE_SERVICE_KEY=
# TUBESCRIBE_WORKER_URL=

# ─── Business Search APIs ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# YELP_API_KEY=
# GOOGLE_PLACES_API_KEY=
# TAVILY_API_KEY=
# BRAVE_SEARCH_API_KEY=

# ─── GitHub / Business Integrations ──────────────────────────────────────────
# GITHUB_TOKEN=
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=
# VAPI_API_KEY=
# VAPI_PHONE_NUMBER_ID=
# VAPI_WEBHOOK_SECRET=
# VAPI_WEBHOOK_URL=

# ─── Telnyx Voice (BuildMyBot inbound calls) ─────────────────────────────────
# Required for the Telnyx ↔ Deepgram voice bridge (inbound BuildMyBot calls).
# DEEPGRAM_API_KEY is also used by the /api/transcribe route above.
# TELNYX_API_KEY=
# TELNYX_WEBHOOK_SECRET=          # shared secret verified on every Telnyx webhook
# TELNYX_OWNER_NUMBER=+1xxxxxxxxxx # E.164 number for transfer_to_owner
# TELNYX_MESSAGING_NUMBER=+1xxxxxxxxxx # Telnyx number that sends the SMS checkout link
# PUBLIC_URL=https://apex.donmatthews.live  # base URL for the stream WebSocket

# ─── Apex Front Desk (Telnyx-hosted AI Assistant) ────────────────────────────
# Backs the "Apex Front Desk" assistant configured directly in the Telnyx
# account (not in this repo — Telnyx hosts the voice/STT/LLM/TTS loop).
# routes/telnyx-assistant.ts is only the custom tool-calling surface it uses.
# TELNYX_ASSISTANT_WEBHOOK_SECRET=  # query-string key verified on every request from the assistant
# APEX_FRONT_DESK_NUMBER=+1xxxxxxxxxx  # the Telnyx number the assistant answers on; also the SMS "from"
# Live-call escalation transfer is configured as a native `transfer` tool
# directly on the Telnyx assistant (a fixed, pre-defined target list Telnyx
# executes itself) — not an Apex-side secret, so there is nothing to set here.

# ─── Live Voice (browser chat call) ──────────────────────────────────────────
# packages/api-server/src/live-voice.ts — the "Live voice call" button in the
# dashboard's Chat window (distinct from the Telnyx-hosted Apex Front Desk and
# Telnyx<->Deepgram phone bridge above). Runs on Deepgram's Voice Agent API
# (STT + LLM + TTS over one websocket) after Gemini Live rejected this
# project's key for its real-time audio endpoint specifically (a documented
# free-tier restriction on that one endpoint, unrelated to a normal Gemini key
# working fine elsewhere). DEEPGRAM_API_KEY is shared with the entries above;
# GROQ_API_KEY backs the "think" (LLM) step via Groq's OpenAI-compatible API,
# since Deepgram requires a bring-your-own endpoint for Groq and Apex holds no
# OpenAI/Anthropic key of its own. GROQ_API_KEY_2, if set, is a dedicated key
# for this feature specifically and takes priority — GROQ_API_KEY is often an
# account-level credential shared with other services, whose per-minute token
# budget a live call can't afford to wait on.
# GROQ_API_KEY=
# GROQ_API_KEY_2=

# ─── Call Bridge (operator call + live AI takeover) ──────────────────────────
# Bridges the operator's own phone with a customer's via a Telnyx conference
# (see routes/call-bridge.ts), with an option to bring the Apex Front Desk
# assistant in live. Needs its own Call Control Application (not the Front
# Desk number's TeXML app) because it drives calls imperatively via the Call
# Control REST API rather than a declarative TeXML/AI-Assistant script.
# TELNYX_CALL_BRIDGE_CONNECTION_ID=  # id of a dedicated Call Control Application
# TELNYX_CALL_BRIDGE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=  # query-string key verified on every call-bridge webhook

PORT=5000
